#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130070} :activity/id #uuid "24e4f3d3-57f6-51a9-afd6-0c3e9a2f8f97" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048225 :topic/id #uuid "893b600f-8843-5755-aed4-951036b65cbd" :topic/math-academy-id 1999} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130398 :tutorial/content "We have explored linear and affine transformations in the plane. We'll now begin to explore nonlinear transformations of plane regions.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a **transformation** on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ We write $$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nRecall that, under this notation,\n\n- $\\mathbf u = [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]$ represents vectors belonging to the input space (i.e., the $uv$-plane), and\n- $\\mathbf x = [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ represents vectors belonging to the output space (i.e., the $xy$-plane).\n\nFor example, consider the following transformation:\n\n$$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = \\mathbf{T}\\left( [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\right) = [\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nGeometrically, this transformation maps the rectangular region in the $uv$-plane below to a circular sector in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc80fe1271d560e9f958eba9454761d56f936567dc439853bc4181cccc3f76f.png)\n\nWe'll explore how to compute the image of a nonlinear transformation in future lessons. But for now, it is sufficient to know that\n\n- $\\mathbf T$ takes points (or vectors) from the $uv$-plane as input and maps them to points (vectors) in the $xy$-plane, and\n- rectangular regions in the $uv$-plane are mapped to regions in the $xy$-plane that are not necessarily parallelograms." :tutorial/id #uuid "c831da3f-8fff-5c5b-8be5-f3602a7303ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4147 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5564d0a2-de60-5f46-b603-c6061aab85f2" :step/math-academy-id 25202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130231}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129193 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129012 :question/id #uuid "28562339-0fe0-5d56-8767-2ec0834525d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6810"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d56cec4a-6121-5988-aaea-478fb6048a6d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049050}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385761}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "838542bc-20ad-57ca-b114-1923593d1021" :step/math-academy-id 25204 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130208}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130208 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129269 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128844 :question/id #uuid "eec994b4-82ac-568c-b493-dc9a21102c2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-11156"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383521}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "0954c2f4-d0e0-56be-8d88-2f803b4f5152" :step/math-academy-id 42421 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130232}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130231 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130466 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation given by\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nWe'll assume that $\\mathbf T$ is a $\\boldsymbol{C}^1$ **transformation**, which means that $x$ and $y$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U$ contained in $\\Bbb R^2.$\n\nThe **Jacobian determinant** of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is defined as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|=\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to the Jacobian determinant simply as the **Jacobian.**\n\nLet's compute the Jacobian determinant of the following transformation:\n\n$$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = \\mathbf{T}\\left( [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\right) = [\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nTo compute the Jacobian determinant of this function, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}v & -u\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}v & u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =\\operatorname{cos}v\\cdot (u\\operatorname{cos}v)-(-u\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ & =u{cos}^{2}v+u{sin}^{2}v \\\\ & =u({cos}^{2}v+{sin}^{2}v) \\\\ & =u.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe'll spend some time discussing the meaning behind the Jacobian shortly. But for now, let's get some practice at computing it." :tutorial/id #uuid "f0ea7366-d3d6-5645-99d8-72f7febb8d19" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7668 :tutorial/title "The Jacobian Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "eadcf9f2-f662-5ba3-8bbc-b9166dfa1142" :step/math-academy-id 44550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130071}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130467 :tutorial/content "To understand what the Jacobian describes, we recall our knowledge of affine transformations.\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the matrix equation\n\n$$\\mathbf x = T\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf b,$ and $\\mathbf x$ are vectors from $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $T$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFor an affine transformation, the determinant of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n- The *area scale factor* of the transformation:\n\nIf a rectangle in the $uv$-plane has area $A,$ its image in the $xy$-plane is a parallelogram of area $|\\det(T)|\\cdot A.$\n\n![](images/04/04f36d98bf7589d1faadeffee0812590337fd36d8bb44f5abe1920948dee5bb0.png)\n- Whether the transformation is *orientation-preserving* or *orientation-reversing:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) > 0,$ the transformation is orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/49/496a49ee541eab31f69f9c571e450547b0c6942b100bc8e24fbd927dd43e4d33.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) < 0,$ the transformation is orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/21/211369d6f3fc70bcf7cf40f3270c24c245d306247efc429cb85589e42c19ee07.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *invertible* or *non-invertible:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) \\neq 0,$ the transformation is invertible.\n\n![](images/e4/e4b840ad5a3efa082ac4056d1d9b7c03d2e3e11e016e053df055dbb2b39f0d1f.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) = 0,$ the transformation is non-invertible. In such cases, a rectangle in the $uv$-plane collapses to either a line segment or point in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9a/9a038c5de0e9f3205194fd7941a4b371c695c51c294f2ad5ac0b8fc1015811d1.png)\n\n![](images/98/9813ba294a0242abbfb885ebd3e82505d05a2f8a1cfa3975c7859f9530cac8b1.png)\n\nAffine transformations are special because these properties are *global*. In other words, they apply *everywhere* in the plane. So, for example, if $\\textrm{det}(T) = 2$, then the image of *every* rectangle in the $uv$-plane is a parallelogram with double the area of its preimage." :tutorial/id #uuid "4dcb1ce1-bfad-524c-a89a-a589532a1067" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7672 :tutorial/title "A Review of Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "c956f456-bd29-5cf7-83bd-de9b757b718f" :step/math-academy-id 44557 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130233}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130468 :tutorial/content "The Jacobian determinant gives information about nonlinear transformations similar to that described by the determinant properties of affine transformations.\n\nThe critical difference is that, in general, the Jacobian describes *local* effects, not global ones.\n\nTo understand what this means, suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation, where $$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nand we assume that $x(u,v)$ and $y(u,v)$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on $U.$\n\nFor ease of notation, let's introduce $J(u_0,v_0)$ to denote the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0, v_0){:}$\n\n$$J(u_0,v_0) = \\left.\\dfrac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(u_0,v_0)}$$\n\nLet's describe $\\mathbf T$'s effect on a *tiny* rectangle in the $uv$-plane whose bottom-left corner is the point $(u_0,v_0).$\n\n![](images/dc/dc084ec02cd0c4fac71379590e8dc4973354cacc898e267a0eb15a6e8500f31f.png)\n\nIt's important to note that the image of our rectangle in the $xy$-plane is typically *not* a parallelogram! However, provided that our rectangle is very small, its image can be *approximated* by a parallelogram (we'll justify this in a future lesson).\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0,v_0)$ describes the following key features between our tiny rectangle and its (approximate) image:\n\n- The *local* area scale factor of $\\mathbf T$.\n\nSuppose the length and width of our rectangle are $\\delta u$ and $\\delta v,$ so its area is $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v.$ Then, it can be shown that the parallelogram in the $xy$-plane has an area equal to $\\left|J(u_0,v_0)\\right| \\cdot \\delta A.$\n\n![](images/be/be1771ebb041c418c58d7d5615510e2fcacfeaf3677fbace739498ef1998ef11.png)\n\nNote the similarity of this formula with that of affine transformations described earlier (we've simply replaced $\\det(T)$ with the Jacobian).\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* orientation-preserving or *locally* orientation-reversing.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)> 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/8c/8c649fdd526a831fb990393b292fb4582dad14c2391d129e9ed59792ebb8ac31.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)< 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/ae/ae5a3d0a42e1c4b9f692994b89815382a30cdf3e676bbd9844258ad2a6961608.png)\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* invertible or *locally* non-invertible.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)\\neq 0,$ the transformation is (locally) invertible.\n\n![](images/33/33ca98206a347e13aa8ec179a52dd755008d9a7d74882391e0c3f2344f6f6641.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)= 0,$ the transformation is (locally) non-invertible.\n\n![](images/3d/3d4534c263a9a5bb26ba35ce8f879cf01db741441d948c023acaa9407d58e6e0.png)\n\n![](images/65/659f26ac6aba808d66a760d5683f265fd7c54431d894b2eabdc255b46368fc24.png)\n\nPoints $(u_0,v_0)$ such that $J(u_0,v_0)=0$ are called **critical points.** Critical points are very important. We'll discuss these in more detail in future lessons.\n\nWe again stress that the Jacobian describes *local* properties, that is, local to the point $(u_0,v_0).$ The effect of a nonlinear transformation on an object varies as we move around the $uv$-plane, so its local properties also vary. So, for example, if a transformation is orientation-preserving at one point, it does *not* mean it's orientation-preserving everywhere!" :tutorial/id #uuid "3f6a2c63-d9fc-533c-8c4b-fd822216f0f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7673 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Jacobian"} :step/id #uuid "0e6a94d3-c32f-5582-98ea-69a8605ef653" :step/math-academy-id 44558 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129288 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128866 :question/id #uuid "32f92fce-b4b9-5e7a-9fe0-255b15edf312" :question/math-academy-id "e-12417"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389222}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "4b57bfb4-8aa6-5afc-b54b-581e471d245a" :step/math-academy-id 44604 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129289 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128867 :question/id #uuid "6102567f-7035-5fa2-b12e-80a0470de4a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12418"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050998}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389215}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"} :step/id #uuid "3dc69ec2-a31b-570c-8cf5-3d69b616fa0a" :step/math-academy-id 44605}] :activity/title "The Jacobian" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129350}]}
